      <h1 class="ed-title">How Thick Should a Treadmill Mat Be?</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        Thickness is the number people compare on mat listings, and it is the one that misleads most often. The right figure depends on the job — floor protection, carpet, or the people living below — and on how firm the material is. Here are the thickness bands, what each one actually does, and why a thick soft mat is worse than a thin firm one.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated October 13, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#thickness-bands" class="btn-ghost">Thickness Bands Table</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          How thick should a treadmill mat be? For most people with a treadmill on a hard
          ground-floor surface, about 1/4 inch of firm PVC or vinyl is enough. For carpet, a
          heavier, denser rubber mat matters more than any particular thickness. For an upstairs
          room where the people below are the concern, look for dense rubber at 3/8 inch or more.
          Beyond that, thickness stops being the useful number and density takes over. This page
          explains each band, why a thick soft mat makes a treadmill less stable rather than
          more, and why the size of the mat should be settled before you think about its
          thickness at all.
        </p>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">How to read the figures on this page.</strong> We review
          treadmills and the kit around them; we have not measured or tested treadmill mats
          ourselves. The thickness bands below are the ranges treadmill mats are commonly sold in,
          and the uses we attach to them are general guidance rather than test results. Where we
          quote a specific mat's thickness, it is a figure from our <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill mat roundup</a>,
          which only prints numbers that more than one source agreed on. Conversions between
          inches and millimetres are simple arithmetic. If a mat is going under a treadmill in a
          flat or above an occupied room, your building's rules and your machine's manual come
          before anything here.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">How Thick Should a Treadmill Mat Be? The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#thickness-bands">Treadmill Mat Thickness Bands and What Each Does</a></li>
            <li><a href="#by-job">Choose the Thickness by the Job, Not the Number</a></li>
            <li><a href="#too-thick-or-soft">Why a Mat That Is Too Thick or Too Soft Makes a Treadmill Unstable</a></li>
            <li><a href="#density">Density Matters More Than Thickness</a></li>
            <li><a href="#rubber-vs-pvc">Rubber vs PVC at the Same Thickness</a></li>
            <li><a href="#size-before-thickness">Size Comes Before Thickness</a></li>
            <li><a href="#hidden-costs">What Extra Thickness Costs You</a></li>
            <li><a href="#walking-pads-and-special-cases">Walking Pads, Stacked Mats and Other Special Cases</a></li>
            <li><a href="#checking-a-mat">How to Check the Thickness and Firmness of the Mat You Bought</a></li>
            <li><a href="#what-to-buy">If You Are Shopping for a Mat</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Thick Should a Treadmill Mat Be? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>For floor protection on a hard floor, around 4 mm to 1/4 inch of firm PVC or
            vinyl is enough; for noise heard in the room below, choose dense rubber at 3/8 inch or
            more.</strong> On carpet, pick a heavy rubber mat for its firmness and grip rather than
            chasing a thickness figure. In every case, a firm mat beats a soft one of the same or
            greater thickness, because a treadmill needs to stand on something that does not give.
          </p>
          <p>
            That answer has three parts because a treadmill mat does three different jobs, and
            each one is sensitive to thickness in a different way. Protecting a floor from dents,
            scuffs and sweat is mostly about coverage; a thin mat that is big enough does it.
            Keeping carpet fibre away from the motor and spreading the machine's weight on a soft
            floor is about firmness and grip. Damping the vibration that travels into the building
            and is heard as a thud downstairs is the only job where extra thickness of the right
            material pays off, and even then it takes the edge off rather than removing the
            problem.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you already know which job you need done, the <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill mats by floor and machine</a> lists a pick for each. If you are not yet sure a mat is worth buying at all, our guide
            to <a href="/do-you-need-a-treadmill-mat/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether you need a treadmill mat</a> covers that question; this page assumes the answer is yes and is only about how thick
            it should be.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="thickness-bands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Treadmill Mat Thickness Bands and What Each Does</h2>
<p>
            Treadmill mats are sold in a fairly narrow range of thicknesses, and they cluster into
            a few bands. The table gives each band in inches and millimetres (1 inch is 25.4 mm,
            so the conversions are arithmetic), the material you usually find at that thickness,
            and the job it is suited to. The uses are general guidance, not measured
            performance.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Thickness</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Usual material</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Good for</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Not good for</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Under 1/8 in. (under 3 mm)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Thin vinyl sheet, runners</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Scuff and sweat barrier on sealed floors</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dents, carpet, noise</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 4 mm to 1/4 in. (4–6.4 mm)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">PVC or vinyl, sometimes PVC foam</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hard ground floors: dents, scuffs, dust, frame rattle</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Noise in the room below</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 3/16 in. (about 4.8 mm), rubber</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Recycled rubber</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Carpet: grip, firmness, fibre barrier</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Serious vibration work</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3/8 to 1/2 in. (9.5–12.7 mm)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dense or vulcanized rubber</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Upstairs rooms, flats, thud heard below</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Tight ceilings, carrying upstairs</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Over 1/2 in. (over 12.7 mm)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Gym flooring, or soft foam</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dense rubber only: dedicated gym floors</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Soft foam at any thickness</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two of the mats in our roundup show where real products sit. The Sunny Health &amp;
            Fitness Floor Protector is listed at 0.16 inches, which is about 4 mm and sits at the
            bottom of the standard PVC band; it is a floor-protection mat and the roundup says
            plainly that it does almost nothing for noise below. The FlooringInc Premium Rubber
            Mat is 3/8 inch of vulcanized rubber, the thickest and densest on that page, and is
            the one chosen for upstairs rooms. The difference between them is not only a little
            over two times the thickness. It is a different material doing a different job.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What a few millimetres looks like</h3>
          <p>
            Listing figures are hard to picture. A US quarter is 1.75 mm thick, so a 4 mm mat is
            a little more than two quarters stacked, a 1/4-inch mat is between three and four,
            and a 3/8-inch mat is a little more than five. That is a useful sense check when a
            mat arrives: if the edge of a "1/4-inch" mat is no thicker than two quarters, it is
            not 1/4 inch.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-job" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Choose the Thickness by the Job, Not the Number</h2>
<p>
            The quickest way to the right thickness is to name the problem you are buying the mat
            to solve. The three common ones need different things, and a mat that is perfect for
            one can be pointless for another.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Protecting a hard floor</h3>
          <p>
            Hardwood, laminate, luxury vinyl and tile all suffer the same way under a treadmill:
            small feet carrying a heavy frame press dents into the surface, the machine creeps a
            little over weeks and scuffs it, and sweat and stray lubricant land on it. A mat
            solves this by spreading the load and putting something sacrificial on top. For that
            job, coverage matters much more than thickness. A properly sized 4 mm to 1/4-inch PVC
            or vinyl mat does it, and going thicker buys little except weight. The one thing to
            avoid is the very thin end: a sheet under about 1/8 inch can stop scuffs but will
            print the shape of the feet onto a soft floor over time.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Carpet</h3>
          <p>
            On carpet, the mat is mostly protecting the treadmill. It keeps carpet fibre away from
            the motor's cooling intake and gives the feet a firmer surface to bear on, so they do
            not sink unevenly into the pile and twist the frame. That favours a heavy, grippy,
            firm mat. A thin rubber mat around 3/16 inch does this well; a thick soft mat does it
            badly, because it adds a second compressible layer on top of the first. Our <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to putting a treadmill on carpet</a> covers the case where carpet is so deep that you need a rigid board under the mat as
            well, which is the real fix for deep pile and soft underlay rather than a thicker
            mat.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The people below</h3>
          <p>
            This is the only job where thickness genuinely earns its keep. The noise heard
            downstairs is mostly vibration that travels from the treadmill's feet into the floor
            structure and is radiated as sound in the room below. A dense, resilient layer between
            the frame and the floor reduces some of it, and more of the right material reduces
            more. That is why 3/8 inch of dense rubber is the usual starting point for an upstairs
            room. Be realistic about the ceiling of what a mat achieves, though: it takes the
            sharp edge off footfalls and stops rattles, and on a lightweight upper floor a running
            treadmill will still be heard. The machine, and whether you walk or run, matter more.
            Our <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet treadmills guide</a> covers that side.
          </p>
          <p>
            Noise you hear in the same room — motor whine, belt sound, the slap of footsteps in
            the air — is a different problem, and no thickness of mat touches it, because that
            sound is not travelling through the mat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="too-thick-or-soft" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Why a Mat That Is Too Thick or Too Soft Makes a Treadmill Unstable</h2>
<p>
            The instinct that more padding must be better is the most common reason people end
            up with the wrong mat. Under a treadmill, softness is a liability, and a thick soft
            mat has more of it to give.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Point loads and uneven sinking</h3>
          <p>
            A treadmill does not rest on its whole footprint. It rests on four to six small feet
            and sometimes a pair of rear rollers, each carrying a share of the frame's weight plus
            the user's. Every footstrike adds a moment of extra load, and it is not shared evenly:
            the foot nearest the landing takes more. On a firm mat, each foot sinks a fraction of
            a millimetre and stays there. On a soft one, each foot sinks further, and by a
            different amount, because the load on each foot is different and changes with every
            stride. The frame ends up very slightly out of level and moving.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What that does to the machine</h3>
          <p>
            A frame that is not level side to side pulls the belt towards the low side, so <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>belt
            tracking</a> that was set correctly drifts. A frame that moves under each step works its
            joints and bolts, and that movement is felt as wobble through the handrails and heard
            as creaks. On a folding machine, the hinge and the lift mechanism take some of that
            movement too. None of this is dramatic on day one, which is why people do not connect
            it to the mat. Our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-shaking-or-wobbling/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that shakes or wobbles</a> lists a soft or uneven base among the first things to check.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Compression that does not come back</h3>
          <p>
            Soft materials under a constant load slowly take on its shape and do not fully
            recover, a behaviour materials engineers call compression set. Under a treadmill that
            means permanent pits under each foot. The pits are rarely the same depth, so the
            machine that was level on the day it went down is not level a few months later.
            Firmer rubber and solid vinyl resist this far better than foamed plastics, which is
            why our roundup excluded thick soft foam tiles and yoga-style exercise mats entirely.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The thumb test</h3>
          <p>
            A simple check before a mat goes under the machine: press a thumb hard into it. A good
            treadmill mat dents slightly at most and springs back at once. If your thumb sinks in
            noticeably, or the dent stays for a few seconds, the mat is too soft to sit under a
            treadmill's feet, whatever its thickness. The same test on the carpet itself tells
            you whether the floor underneath needs a rigid board.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="density" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Density Matters More Than Thickness</h2>
<p>
            Density is how much material is packed into a given volume. Two mats of the same
            thickness can differ enormously in it: a solid rubber or vinyl sheet is all material,
            while a foamed PVC or EVA mat is full of tiny air cells, which is what makes it light,
            cheap and soft. For a treadmill, the solid end is what you want, because density is
            what resists indentation and what absorbs vibration rather than simply compressing
            under it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Most mat listings do not quote density, but you can estimate it from figures they do
            give. Divide the mat's weight by its area to get pounds per square foot. Then compare
            that across mats of similar thickness. As illustrative arithmetic, not real products:
            a 4 x 6-foot mat covers 24 square feet, so if one weighs 12 lb it carries half a pound
            per square foot, and if another of the same thickness weighs 36 lb it carries a pound
            and a half. The second has three times as much material in the same space, and it
            will behave very differently under a treadmill's feet.
          </p>
          <p>
            Weight is also a practical signal on its own. Our mat roundup notes that a 4 x 6-foot
            rubber mat at 3/8 inch weighs several times what a vinyl mat of the same size does,
            and that a large recycled-rubber mat is listed at roughly 30 lb or more. That weight
            is the density doing its job, and it is also why a dense mat stays put on carpet
            without adhesive. A mat that weighs very little for its size is almost certainly
            foamed, and should be judged as a floor-protection layer rather than a vibration
            one.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more reason to lean on density rather than the thickness figure: listed
            thicknesses are unreliable. Our roundup found two of its five mats described with
            different thicknesses by different sellers, and owners of budget PVC mats have
            reported measuring them thinner than advertised. Weight and material are harder to
            misstate, and you can check both the day the mat arrives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rubber-vs-pvc" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Rubber vs PVC at the Same Thickness</h2>
<p>
            Almost every treadmill mat is PVC (vinyl) or rubber, and at the same nominal thickness
            they are not equivalent. The table summarises how they compare for the jobs on this
            page; the <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">mat roundup</a> covers smell, floor compatibility and specific picks in more detail.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">At the same thickness</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">PVC / vinyl</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Rubber</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Light, easy to roll and move</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Heavy, hard to carry upstairs</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Resisting dents</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fair; foamed PVC dents under heavy feet</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Better</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Vibration damping</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Limited</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Better, and improves with thickness</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Grip on carpet</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Can creep</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stays put</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Smell when new</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Budget PVC sometimes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Often, especially recycled crumb rubber</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Typical role</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hard ground floors</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Carpet and upstairs rooms</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The practical consequence is that thickness figures only compare fairly within a
            material. A 1/4-inch PVC mat and a 1/4-inch rubber mat are different products, and
            the rubber one will usually do more vibration work and dent less. Within PVC, solid
            vinyl sheet is firmer than foamed PVC of the same thickness. If a listing describes a
            PVC mat as "foam" or "cushioned", treat it as a floor-protection layer only.
          </p>
          <p>
            A note on smell, because it affects which thickness you can live with: thicker rubber
            means more rubber, and usually more of the new-rubber smell. Plan to air a thick
            rubber mat in a garage or outdoors for several days before it goes into a bedroom or a
            closed room. Vulcanized rubber is generally sold as lower-odour than loose-bound crumb
            rubber.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="size-before-thickness" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Size Comes Before Thickness</h2>
<p>
            If you only have the budget or patience to get one dimension right, get the size
            right. A mat that is thick enough but too small fails at the edges, which is exactly
            where the treadmill's feet, rear rollers and motor intake are. A thinner mat that
            extends well beyond the machine on every side protects the floor and keeps dust and
            fibre away from the motor, which are the jobs most people actually need done.
          </p>
          <p>
            Measure the machine, not the belt. The belt size is on the box, and it is always
            smaller than the frame: a 20 x 60-inch belt can sit on a machine 35 inches wide and 75
            inches long or more. Use the assembled footprint from the manual, measured unfolded,
            and add a few inches on every side and more at the front and back. In practice that
            usually means about 30 x 60 inches for a compact <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>walking pad</a>, 36 x 78 inches for a
            typical folding treadmill, and 36 x 90 inches or a 48-inch-wide mat for long or wide
            machines. The mat sits inside the safety clearance the manual asks for behind the
            machine; it does not replace it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Size and thickness also interact in one awkward way. Thick rubber mats tend to come in
            smaller sheets, because a large sheet of thick rubber is very heavy. The FlooringInc
            mat in our roundup, at 3/8 inch, is 72 inches long, which is shorter than many
            full-size treadmills. If the thick mat you want is not long enough, two mats butted
            end to end work, provided the join does not sit under a foot or a roller, where it
            will lift and curl over time. Do not solve the problem by buying a thinner mat that
            fits and stacking a smaller thick one on top under the motor end; the step between
            them puts the frame on two different heights.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hidden-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Extra Thickness Costs You</h2>
<p>
            Thicker is never free, and the costs are worth weighing before you pay for a heavy
            mat you may not need.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Deck height and headroom.</strong> Every millimetre of mat raises the
            running surface by the same amount. On its own a 3/8-inch mat is a small change, but
            the clearance above your head at the top of your stride, with the deck inclined, is
            often tighter than people expect in basements and loft rooms. Add the mat to the
            deck height when you do that arithmetic.</li>
            <li><strong>Weight.</strong> A thick rubber mat can weigh several times what a vinyl
            mat of the same size does. That matters if it has to go up a flight of stairs, and
            it matters every time you want to move the treadmill to clean under it.</li>
            <li><strong>Edges.</strong> A thick mat has a thick edge, and a 1/2-inch lip around
            the machine is something to catch a toe on when you step off, especially at the back
            where you dismount. Bevelled edges help; so does keeping the mat margin clear of
            anything you will walk across often.</li>
            <li><strong>Smell and cost.</strong> More rubber means more of the new-rubber smell
            and a higher price. Going by the price ranges in our mat roundup, the dense 3/8-inch
            rubber pick costs roughly two to three times the standard vinyl one.</li>
            <li><strong>Folding clearance.</strong> On a folding treadmill, check that the deck
            still folds and the transport wheels still roll with the extra height under the
            feet. It is a thirty-second check, and easier before the mat is cut or
            returned.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of these is a reason to avoid a thick mat in an upstairs flat. They are reasons
            not to buy one for a ground-floor room where a 1/4-inch vinyl mat does the whole
            job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-pads-and-special-cases" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Walking Pads, Stacked Mats and Other Special Cases</h2>
<p>
            A few situations come up often enough to answer directly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walking pads.</strong> A walking pad is lighter than a treadmill and is used
            at walking speeds, so the loads are smaller and the floor-protection job is easier. A
            standard 4 mm to 1/4-inch mat is generally enough on a hard floor. The one extra check
            is airflow: some pads sit very low and draw cooling air from underneath, so look at
            where the vents are and avoid any soft, deep-pile or cushioned surface that could sag
            up against them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Stacking two mats.</strong> Two thin firm mats stacked are roughly as good as
            one thicker mat of the same material, and better than one soft one. Two soft mats
            stacked are worse than either alone. If you stack, use the same material for both
            layers and make sure no join in either layer falls under a foot.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Mat plus board on carpet.</strong> On deep carpet, the standard build-up is a
            rigid board — plywood or similar, cut to the footprint plus a margin — laid under a
            firm mat. The board provides the
            flatness; the mat provides grip and a dust barrier. A thicker mat is not a substitute
            for the board.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Interlocking tiles.</strong> Soft EVA foam tiles fail the thumb test and are
            a poor base for a treadmill at any thickness. Firm, high-density rubber gym tiles are
            a different product and work well, provided no foot sits on a seam; our <a href="/best-gym-flooring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym flooring guide</a> covers which thickness suits weights, cardio and bodyweight areas.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Existing rubber gym flooring.</strong> If the floor is already dense rubber, it
            is doing what a mat would do. Another layer adds little.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="checking-a-mat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Check the Thickness and Firmness of the Mat You Bought</h2>
<p>
            Because listing thicknesses are unreliable, it is worth five minutes checking the mat
            when it arrives, before the treadmill goes on it and the return window closes.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Measure the edge.</strong> A ruler with millimetre marks is enough; a
            cheap set of calipers is better. Measure in three or four places along the edge,
            because some mats are thinner in the middle of a roll than at the ends. The
            quarter-coin comparison in the bands section works as a rough check if you have
            nothing else.</li>
            <li><strong>Weigh it if you can.</strong> Stand on a bathroom scale holding the rolled
            mat and subtract your own weight. Divide by the area for a pounds-per-square-foot
            figure, and compare with what the listing implied.</li>
            <li><strong>Press it.</strong> The thumb test again: firm, quick recovery, no lasting
            dent.</li>
            <li><strong>Let it flatten.</strong> Unroll it curl side down in a warm room and
            weigh the ends for a day or two. A curl trapped under a treadmill foot becomes a
            permanent ridge.</li>
            <li><strong>Level the machine on it.</strong> Once the treadmill is in place, check
            level side to side and front to back, and check again after a couple of weeks, when
            the mat and any carpet underneath have settled under the load.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If the mat is noticeably thinner than advertised or soft enough to fail the thumb
            test, return it rather than living with it. A mat that lets the frame move is doing
            harm, and the harm is slow enough that you will not notice until the belt starts to
            wander.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>If You Are Shopping for a Mat</h2>
<p>
            Pulling it together, the order of decisions is: size first, then the job, then the
            material, and thickness last, because the job and the material mostly decide it for
            you.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Hard ground floor, no one below:</strong> a firm PVC or vinyl mat of
            roughly 4 mm to 1/4 inch, sized beyond the machine on every side.</li>
            <li><strong>Carpet:</strong> a heavy rubber mat that grips and does not compress,
            with a rigid board underneath if the pile or underlay is deep.</li>
            <li><strong>Upstairs or over an occupied room:</strong> dense rubber at 3/8 inch or
            more, accepting that it reduces the thud rather than removing it.</li>
            <li><strong>Walking pad:</strong> a compact standard mat, with the vents kept
            clear.</li>
            <li><strong>Avoid at any thickness:</strong> soft foam tiles, <a href="/best-yoga-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>yoga mats</a> and anything
            sold as cushioning.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">roundup of treadmill mats matched to floor and machine</a> has a pick for each of these cases, with the sizes and thicknesses we could confirm
            and the caveats on each. If you are setting up a new machine more broadly, the <a href="/best-treadmill-accessories/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill accessories guide</a> puts the mat alongside the lubricant, fan and heart-rate strap, and explains why the
            mat is worth laying before the treadmill arrives rather than after.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes With Treadmill Mat Thickness</h2>
          <p>Most thickness mistakes come from treating the mat like a cushion. Under a treadmill it is a base, and a base should be firm.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying the thickest, softest mat on the page</h3>
              <p>Soft foam compresses unevenly under a treadmill's feet, so the frame rocks and the belt drifts. It also takes a permanent set, leaving pits that throw the machine out of level. Press a thumb into any mat before it goes down: if it sinks or the dent lingers, it is too soft for a treadmill.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying for thickness when the job is floor protection</h3>
              <p>On a hard ground floor with nobody below, a firm 4 mm to 1/4-inch mat that extends past the machine does the whole job. A heavy 3/8-inch rubber mat there adds weight, cost and smell for a problem you do not have. Spend the difference on a bigger mat instead.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting the listed thickness</h3>
              <p>Mat listings are inconsistent, and some budget mats arrive thinner than advertised. Measure the edge in several places when the mat arrives, weigh it if you can, and compare with the listing. If it is materially thinner or softer than described, return it before the treadmill is sitting on it.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Choosing thickness before size</h3>
              <p>A thick mat that stops short of the treadmill's feet, rollers or motor intake fails exactly where it matters. Measure the assembled machine unfolded, add a margin on every side, and choose a mat that size first. If the thick mat you want is too short, butt two together with the join away from any foot.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">About 1/4 inch of firm vinyl protects a hard floor; dense rubber at
            3/8 inch or more is for the room below.</strong> On carpet, choose a heavy, firm rubber
            mat for its grip rather than its thickness, and add a board if the pile is deep.
          </p>
          <p>
            Size the mat to the machine before you think about thickness, judge it by density rather
            than the number on the listing, and never put a soft mat under a treadmill. For
            mats sorted by floor and machine, see our <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best treadmill mats roundup</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

