        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How thick should a treadmill mat be?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For floor protection on a hard ground floor, around 4 mm to 1/4 inch of firm PVC or vinyl is enough. For noise heard in the room below, choose dense rubber at 3/8 inch or more. On carpet, a heavy, firm rubber mat matters more than the exact thickness. A thick soft mat is worse than a thinner firm one under any treadmill.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a thicker treadmill mat better?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only if it is also dense. Extra thickness of firm rubber does more vibration work, which helps in an upstairs room. Extra thickness of soft foam lets the treadmill's feet sink unevenly, so the frame rocks and the belt drifts off centre. For protecting a hard floor, thickness beyond about 1/4 inch adds weight and cost for little benefit.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What thickness of treadmill mat do I need for an upstairs room?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Dense rubber at around 3/8 to 1/2 inch is the usual range for reducing the thud heard downstairs. Expect it to take the edge off footfalls and stop rattles rather than make a running treadmill silent. On a lightweight upper floor, walking instead of running and choosing a quieter machine matter more than any mat.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can a treadmill mat be too thick?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, in two ways. A soft mat that is too thick lets the machine move and sink unevenly, which affects belt tracking and stability. And any thick mat raises the deck, adds weight, and leaves a raised edge to trip on when you step off. Thick only makes sense for dense rubber in a room where vibration is the problem.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is 4 mm thick enough for a treadmill mat?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For protecting a hard ground floor from dents, scuffs and sweat, a firm 4 mm mat that is large enough is generally adequate, and many standard treadmill mats sit at about that thickness. It will do very little for noise in a room below, and a foamed 4 mm mat may dent permanently under a heavy machine's feet.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does a rubber mat need to be thicker than a PVC mat?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not necessarily. At the same thickness, rubber is usually denser, grips better and damps more vibration than PVC, so a thin rubber mat around 3/16 inch already suits carpet well. Rubber only needs to be thicker, around 3/8 inch or more, when the goal is reducing the vibration heard in the room below.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

